<p>Parsing CSV files is a common task. What are our options? We&rsquo;ll start
with <em>cl-csv</em>, add the <em>data-table</em> library, and present <em>lisp-stat</em>&rsquo;s data frames.</p>

<p><em>This is another contribution to the Common Lisp Cookbook so <a href="https://lispcookbook.github.io/cl-cookbook/csv.html">you should read it there</a> as this is where it will get updates.</em></p>

<p>With a very simple dataset we could split CSV lines manually (<code>subseq</code>, <code>split-sequence</code>,
<code>str:split</code>, <code>cl-ppcre</code>...) but when a column contains the separator,
a library starts to be useful.</p>

<p>And a library will be even more so to help us manipulate columns by name,
to use tabular data structures, to automatically
infer the columns&rsquo; types, and more.</p>

<p>I recommend:</p>

<ul>
<li>for simple use, try <code>cl-csv</code></li>
<li>if you&rsquo;ll do statistics or want a good dataframes library, go for <code>lisp-stats</code></li>
<li>to work with large datasets, see <code>cl-csv</code>&rsquo;s <code>:row-fn</code> argument.</li>
<li>for the fastest parsing, maybe <code>fare-csv</code>, or DuckDB with
<a href="https://github.com/ak-coram/cl-duckdb">cl-duckdb</a>, which can also
ingest <a href="https://duckdb.org/docs/2.0/data/data_sources">much more file
formats</a> than CSV,
including Excel.</li>
</ul>




