<h1 id="introduction-our-test-files">Introduction: our test files</h1>

<p>Let&rsquo;s work together with this simple CSV file, <code>persons.csv</code>:</p>

<pre><code class="language-txt">Alice,7,1
Bob,77,0
</code></pre>

<p>The separator is <code>,</code> (often the default in libraries).</p>

<p>For simplicity, the file doesn&rsquo;t contain a headers row.</p>

<p>This second file, <code>persons2.csv</code>, contains headers:</p>

<pre><code class="language-txt">name,age,lisper
Alice,7,1
Bob,77,0
</code></pre>

<h1 id="parsing-with-cl-csv">Parsing with cl-csv</h1>

<p>The <a href="https://github.com/AccelerationNet/cl-csv">cl-csv</a> library is on Quicklisp.</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(ql:quicklisp &quot;cl-csv&quot;)
</code></pre>

<p>It allows to parse data from a string, from a file, or from any stream.</p>

<p>It may not be the fastest, but it has a decent set of quality-of-life
features.</p>

<p>Please go read its README, as we don&rsquo;t illustrate some of its features
here. For example, it supports multiline quoted data, we can set the
quote character, it has good error messages (line and column number,
char index), etc.</p>

<p>For reference, this is the function signature of <code>cl-csv:read-csv</code> (<code>C-c-s</code> on Slime):</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(cl-csv:read-csv stream-or-string :csv-reader csv-reader
                                             :row-fn row-fn
                                             :map-fn map-fn
                                             :data-map-fn data-map-fn
                                             :sample sample
                                             :skip-first-p skip-first-p
                                             :separator *separator*
                                             :quote *quote*
                                             :escape *quote-escape*
                                             :unquoted-empty-string-is-nil *unquoted-empty-string-is-nil*
                                             :quoted-empty-string-is-nil *quoted-empty-string-is-nil*
                                             :trim-outer-whitespace *trim-outer-whitespace*
                                             :newline *read-newline*
                                             :escape-mode *escape-mode*)
</code></pre>

<h2 id="parsing-from-a-string">Parsing from a string</h2>

<p>To parse from a string, just use <code>cl-csv:read-csv</code>:</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(cl-csv:read-csv &quot;Alice,7,1
Bob,77,0&quot;)
;; =&gt;
((&quot;Alice&quot; &quot;7&quot; &quot;1&quot;) (&quot;Bob&quot; &quot;77&quot; &quot;0&quot;))
</code></pre>

<p>It returns a list of lists, values are all strings.</p>

<h2 id="parsing-from-a-file">Parsing from a file</h2>

<p>Also use <code>cl-csv:read-csv</code> but give <em>a pathname</em> as argument, not only
the filename as a string.</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(cl-csv:read-csv #P&quot;persons.csv&quot;)
;; =&gt;
((&quot;Alice,7,1&quot;) (&quot;Bob,77,0&quot;))
</code></pre>

<p>What happens if you omit the <code>#P</code>?</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(cl-csv:read-csv &quot;persons.csv&quot;)
;; =&gt;
((&quot;persons.csv&quot;))
</code></pre>

<p>this looks strange and no, there is no bug ;)</p>

<p>To parse from a stream, see the library&rsquo;s documentation.</p>

<h2 id="using-another-separator">Using another separator</h2>

<p>Is <code>;</code> the columns separator? Use <code>cl-csv:read-csv</code>&rsquo;s <code>:separator</code>
argument, or the global <code>cl-csv:*separator*</code> parameter.</p>

<p>For a Tab, use <code>#\Tab</code>.</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(defparameter *csv-string* &quot;Alice;7;1
Bob;77;0&quot;)

(cl-csv:read-csv *csv-string* :separator #\;)

;; OR

(let ((cl-csv:*separator* #\;))
  (cl-csv:read-csv *csv-string*))
</code></pre>

<h2 id="skipping-the-first-row-of-headers">Skipping the first row of headers</h2>

<p>With <code>cl-csv</code>, use <code>:skip-first-p</code>.</p>

<p>However, we&rsquo;ll use this row of headers as soon as we work with dataframes.</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(cl-csv:read-csv &quot;name,age,lisper
Alice,7,1
Bob,77,0&quot;
 :skip-first-p t)
</code></pre>

<h2 id="returning-a-list-of-objects">Returning a list of objects</h2>

<p>So far, we got a list of <em>lists</em> with raw values as strings. We can do
whatever we want with them. <code>cl-csv</code> has a <code>:map-fn</code> argument that
we can use to create an object while parsing each row.</p>

<p>Let&rsquo;s re-use our class definition from the CLOS chapter:</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(defclass person ()
  ((name :initarg :name :initform &quot;&quot;)
   (age :initarg :age :initform nil)
   (lisper :initarg :lisper :initform nil)))
</code></pre>

<p>We can parse our CSV and return a list of objects at once:</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(cl-csv:read-csv #p&quot;persons.csv&quot;
  :map-fn (lambda (values)
             (make-instance 'person :name (nth 0 values)
                                    :age (parse-integer (nth 1 values))
                                    :lisper (if (equal &quot;1&quot; (nth 2 values)) t nil))))
</code></pre>

<p>Observe that:</p>

<ul>
<li>we had to parse the age from a string to an integer ourselves</li>
<li>our file doesn&rsquo;t have a headers row.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="streaming-reads-for-large-files">Streaming reads (for large files)</h2>

<p><code>cl-csv</code> provides the <code>:row-fn</code> parameter, that <em>does something</em> with
each row and <em>doesn&rsquo;t return them</em>. This allows us to work with large
datasets.</p>

<p>In this example, the <code>:row-fn</code> function has <em>side-effects</em> after each
row was read and passed to it. Our function returns a result, the mean
of ages.</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(let ((sum-ages 0)
      (nb-ages 0))
  (cl-csv:read-csv #p&quot;persons.csv&quot;
                   :row-fn (lambda (values)
                             (incf sum-ages (parse-integer (nth 1 values)))
                             (incf nb-ages)))
  ;; Age mean:
  (/ sum-ages nb-ages))
</code></pre>

