<h1 id="cl-csv-and-data-table-column-names-column-types-columns-manipulation">cl-csv and data-table: column names, column types, columns manipulation</h1>

<p><a href="https://github.com/AccelerationNet/data-table">data-table</a> is a
library by the same authors that brings a data structure that
represents our CSV data.</p>

<p>A data table has rows of data, associates names to the columns, and guesses columns types.</p>

<p>Then, we can access values by row and column names, and manipulate columns (add data, take a substract etc).</p>

<p>We link cl-csv and data-tables together with the system <code>cl-csv-data-table</code> (defined in <code>cl-csv</code>).</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(ql:quickload '(&quot;cl-csv&quot; &quot;data-table&quot; &quot;cl-csv-data-table&quot;))
</code></pre>

<p>You don&rsquo;t need to go read its README, as it is poorly documented (only in tests).</p>

<p>We&rsquo;ll use the second CSV file, <code>persons2.csv</code>.</p>

<p>Let&rsquo;s parse it:</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(cl-csv:get-data-table-from-csv #p&quot;persons2.csv&quot;)
;; =&gt;
#&lt;DATA-TABLE:DATA-TABLE {1205D151C3}&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>This returns a <code>DATA-TABLE</code> object. What is it? We can inspect it:</p>

<pre><code class="language-txt">(inspect *)

The object is a STANDARD-OBJECT of type DATA-TABLE:DATA-TABLE.
0. COLUMN-NAMES: (&quot;name&quot; &quot;age&quot; &quot;lisper&quot;)
1. COLUMN-TYPES: (STRING INTEGER INTEGER)
2. ROWS: ((&quot;Alice&quot; 7 1) (&quot;Bob&quot; 77 0))
</code></pre>

<p>We observe:</p>

<ul>
<li>the data-table used our headers row</li>
<li>the columns&rsquo; types were inferred: &ldquo;name&rdquo; is a string, &ldquo;age&rdquo; and
&ldquo;lisper&rdquo; are integers.</li>
<li>in each row, the &ldquo;age&rdquo; and &ldquo;lisper&rdquo; fields were parsed to an integer.</li>
</ul>

<p>Let&rsquo;s save our result to a parameter:</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(defparameter *dt* **)
</code></pre>

<p>Get all rows with:</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(data-table:rows *dt*)
</code></pre>

<p>Instead of accessing a column by its index, you can access it by its name.</p>

<p>Use <code>data-table:data-table-value dt &amp;key row row-idx col-name col-idx</code>.</p>

<p>For example, what is the &ldquo;name&rdquo; of the first row?</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(csv:data-table-value *dt* :row-idx 0 :col-name &quot;age&quot;)
;; =&gt; 7
</code></pre>

<p>The library has more functions to manipulate columns and
data-tables. We give them as a reference, and then we&rsquo;ll see how lisp-stat does it.</p>

<p><code>data-table:number-of-rows</code> and <code>data-table:number-of-columns</code></p>

<p><code>data-table-to-plists</code>, <code>plists-to-data-table</code></p>

<p>Add columns together with <code>(csv:add-column dt column-name [default] [column-type] [index])</code>.</p>

<p>Select columns with <code>(csv:select-columns table column-names)</code> to create a new data-table.</p>

<p>In this example, we create a new data-table object with only 2
columns. We also inverted their order.</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(csv:select-columns *dt* '(&quot;age&quot; &quot;name&quot;))
;; =&gt;
#&lt;DATA-TABLE:DATA-TABLE {1203F88213}&gt;
CL-USER&gt; (inspect *)

The object is a STANDARD-OBJECT of type DATA-TABLE:DATA-TABLE.
0. COLUMN-NAMES: (&quot;age&quot; &quot;name&quot;)
1. COLUMN-TYPES: (INTEGER STRING)
2. ROWS: ((7 &quot;Alice&quot;) (77 &quot;Bob&quot;))
&gt; q
</code></pre>

<p><code>overlay-region  new dt :col-idx col-idx :row-idx row-idx)</code>: &ldquo;puts all values from new-dt into dt starting at row-idx col-idx&rdquo;.</p>

<p><code>make-sub-table parent :uri uri :lri lri :uci uci :lci lci</code>:</p>

<pre><code class="language-txt">Make a new data table from a subset of another data-table
   lci - low column index
   uci - upper column index (as per subseq 1 beyond the col you want)
   lri - low row index
   uri - upper row index (as per subseq 1 beyond the row you want)
</code></pre>

<p>Write a data-table to a file (or any stream) with <code>(data-table-to-csv dt &amp;optional stream)</code></p>

<p>All this is good, but today lisp-stat does this, and more.</p>

<h1 id="lisp-stat-s-data-frame">lisp-stat&rsquo;s Data Frame</h1>

<p><a href="https://lisp-stat.dev/">lisp-stat</a> is a powerful library.</p>

<blockquote>
<p>Lisp-Stat provides a statistical computing engine comparable to R, Python, Julia and Matlab. Its major differentiators are a true REPL based development environment, high performance and a single binary image that can be moved through the devops pipeline in a secure, safe and manageable way.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>It integrates with Jupyter notebooks, to Quarto, it can load R datasets...</p>

<p>It&rsquo;s pretty good and actively used and developed.</p>

<p>It interests us for its <a href="https://lisp-stat.dev/docs/manuals/data-frame/">Data
Frame</a>. You should go
read its documentation ;)</p>

<p>Let&rsquo;s load the library:</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(ql:quickload :lisp-stat)
</code></pre>

<p>We&rsquo;ll give a brief showcase. For this, we&rsquo;ll go to the <code>ls-user</code> package.
It is &ldquo;the package for you to do statistics work&rdquo;.</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(in-package :ls-user)
</code></pre>

<p>We can read a CSV file with <code>read-csv</code>. This function is actually not
the one we used so far with cl-csv, it is a wrapper around <code>fare-csv</code>.</p>

<p>So, using another separator is different, use <code>fare-csv:*separator*</code>.</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(defparameter *persons-df* (read-csv #p&quot;persons2.csv&quot;))
;; =&gt; *PERSONS-DF*
</code></pre>

<p>It is important we used our file <code>persons2.csv</code> which contains the
first row with column names.</p>

<p>We can start working with our data.</p>

<p>How do we get all names of our dataset? Simply like this:</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">LS-USER&gt; (column *persons-df* 'name)
#(&quot;Alice&quot; &quot;Bob&quot;)
</code></pre>

<p>That&rsquo;s already interesting, isn&rsquo;t it?</p>

<p>Observe that:
- the result is not a list, it is an array.</p>

<p>lisp-stat has a notation trick to access data in an even shorter
way. For this, we need to define an &ldquo;environment&rdquo; with <code>defdf</code>:</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">LS-USER&gt; (defdf persons (read-csv #p&quot;persons2.csv&quot;))
;; =&gt; #&lt;DATA-FRAME (2 observations of 3 variables)&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>Observe that:
- we deliberately didn&rsquo;t use &ldquo;*earmuffs*&rdquo; around &ldquo;persons&rdquo;
- the environment already gives us information: it saw 2 rows and 3 columns.</p>

<p>Now, we can get all names simply with:</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">LS-USER&gt; persons:name   &lt;----- you can use auto-completion, of course.
;; =&gt; #(&quot;Alice&quot; &quot;Bob&quot;)
</code></pre>

<p>all ages:</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">LS-USER&gt; persons:age
;; =&gt; #(7 77)
</code></pre>

<p>We can inspect the shape of our data:</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">LS-USER&gt; (head persons)

;;   NAME  AGE LISPER
;; 0 Alice   7      1
;; 1 Bob    77      0
</code></pre>

<p>Using another function, <code>select</code>, we can get all ages with:</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">LS-USER&gt; (select persons t 'age)
;;                      ^^^ take the age of all rows
</code></pre>

<p>But with <code>select</code> we can get a subset of values:</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">LS-USER&gt; (select persons '(0 1) 'age)
;;                         ^^^ our subset (it's all our data lol)
;; =&gt; #(7 77)
</code></pre>

<p>We can get values of some columns:</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">LS-USER&gt; (columns persons '(age name))
;; =&gt; #(#(7 77) #(&quot;Alice&quot; &quot;Bob&quot;))
</code></pre>

<p>We can get a high-level description of our dataset:</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">LS-USER&gt; (describe persons)
PERSONS
  A data-frame with 2 observations of 3 variables

Variable | Type | Unit | Label
-------- | ---- | ---- | -----------
NAME     | NIL  | NIL  | NIL
AGE      | NIL  | NIL  | NIL
LISPER   | NIL  | NIL  | NIL
</code></pre>

<p>This shows that the columns&rsquo; types were not inferred. Can we fix this?</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">LS-USER&gt; (heuristicate-types persons)
NIL

LS-USER&gt; (describe persons)
PERSONS
  A data-frame with 2 observations of 3 variables

Variable | Type    | Unit | Label
-------- | ----    | ---- | -----------
NAME     | STRING  | NIL  | NIL
AGE      | INTEGER | NIL  | NIL
LISPER   | BIT     | NIL  | NIL
</code></pre>

<p>Yes, now they are.</p>

<p>We can now manipulate columns: add, remove, rename, replace, transform... as well as rows: add, count, loop over (do-rows), map into, mask, filter...</p>

<p>Everything is in lisp-stat&rsquo;s documentation!</p>

<h1 id="utilities">Utilities</h1>

<h2 id="print-data-in-an-ascii-table">Print data in an ascii table</h2>

<p>We saw how to do that with lisp-stat&rsquo;s <code>head</code> function.</p>

<p>We have another solution with the <a href="https://github.com/vindarel/cl-ansi-term">cl-ansi-term</a> library.</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(ql:quickload '(&quot;cl-ansi-term&quot;))
</code></pre>

<p>Its global nickname is <code>term</code> (warn: global nicknames are now legacy,
prefer package-local nicknames).</p>

<p>We use <code>term:table</code> to print our tabular data.</p>

<pre><code class="language-lisp">(let ((csv (cl-csv:read-csv #p&quot;persons2.csv&quot;)))
  (term:table csv))

;; =&gt;
+-----+---+------+
|name |age|lisper|
+-----+---+------+
|Alice|7  |1     |
+-----+---+------+
|Bob  |77 |0     |
+-----+---+------+
</code></pre>

<p>It has a few more options. With many columns, you could display only a
chosen set of columns. You can style cells programmatically, etc.</p>

